<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Portuguese Gold Coast</h1><p class="st-meta">1482 CE – 1642 CE · capital Elmina</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Portugal's chain of trading forts on the Gold Coast, centred on Sao Jorge da Mina, which for over a century channelled West African gold to Lisbon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Portuguese ships reached the gold-trading coast of present-day Ghana in 1471. In 1482, on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-portugal">King John II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diogo-de-azambuja">Diogo de Azambuja</a> built the fortress of Sao Jorge da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina</a>, after negotiating with a local chief remembered as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caramansa">Caramansa</a>. Smaller forts followed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axim">Axim</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shama-ghana">Shama</a> and other points.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The forts were run by a royal monopoly whose governor bought gold from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> traders with cloth, brass, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and slaves brought from elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. At its height the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a> trade supplied a large share of Portugal's gold. The garrisons were small and depended on agreements with coastal rulers such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-eguafo">Kingdom of Eguafo</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, rivals in the trade, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina</a> in 1637 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axim">Axim</a> in 1642, ending Portuguese power on the coast. Their posts became the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-gold-coast">Dutch Gold Coast</a>, and Elmina Castle is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/portuguese-gold-coast.jpg" alt="Portuguese Gold Coast" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: myself, based on ancient national symbol. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Portugal_(1640).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1482 CE – 1642 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Elmina</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Diogo de Azambuja</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Portuguese Mina, Sao Jorge da Mina</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1562&amp;focus=portuguese-gold-coast" aria-label="Portuguese Gold Coast on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4643 1987 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Portuguese Gold Coast lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4963 2243L4773 1044M4963 2243L4965 2242"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4773 1044h0M4965 2242h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4963 2243h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4963 2243h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1562&amp;focus=portuguese-gold-coast">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

